def rawMdx : DocSlug -> String
  | .introduction => "---\ntitle: \"What Lakeport is (and is not)\"\nsummary: \"Why Lakeport fits local-first and client-heavy apps, what you write day to day, and how the four parts of this manual fit together.\"\nsection: \"Getting started\"\norder: 1\nreadingMinutes: 6\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# What Lakeport is (and is not)\n\nLakeport is a client application framework built on Lean 4 and compiled to WebAssembly (with native desktop and mobile shells in active development). You write component views in JSX-style `.lp` files and styles in regular `.css` files, state your critical client rules in plain English, and let your coding agent maintain the Lean state logic and proofs beside them.\n\nYou do not need prior Lean experience to adopt Lakeport, and you do not need Lean on your backend.\n\n## Where Lakeport pays off most: local-first and client-heavy apps\n\nIn a traditional server-rendered or thin CRUD web app, most business rules live on the backend inside database transactions. If a browser only renders forms and posts them to a server, proving theorems on the client buys very little.\n\nLakeport benefits **local-first** and client-state-heavy software more than almost any other frontend stack, because real business logic lives on the user's device:\n\n- **Offline sync queues and outboxes:** Edits accumulate while disconnected, replay across flaky connections, and reconcile when responses arrive out of order.\n- **Optimistic UI and collaborative state:** Local mutations apply immediately and must converge cleanly with server acknowledgements without duplicating or dropping items.\n- **Stateful checkouts and idempotent retries:** Client retry loops must keep in-flight payment or reservation tokens locked when Wi-Fi drops mid-request.\n- **Long-lived tabs and Service Worker caches:** Frontend bundles cached on a device continue talking to Go, Node, Python, or Rust backends while schemas evolve across deploys.\n\nWhenever bugs hide in state transitions rather than pixel alignment, Lean's exhaustive pattern matching and zero-byte compile-time theorems catch mistakes that happy-path unit tests miss.\n\n## What you read and edit vs. what your agent writes\n\n| Concern | File type | Who typically writes it | Runtime footprint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Component markup & layout | `src/*.lp` | You or your coding agent | Lowered to `StyledView` and compiled to Wasm |\n| Visual tokens & layout rules | `src/*.css` | You or your coding agent | Static `bundle.css` (and `.lpcss` for native shells) |\n| Client state, JSON decoders & reducers | `src/*.lean` | Your coding agent, guided in plain English | Compiled to C11 and `wasm32` |\n| Invariants & regression laws | `src/*Laws.lean` | Your coding agent via `lakeport mcp` | **0 bytes** (`[proof-only, 0 B C]`) |\n\n## Honest platform status\n\nLakeport separates what is production-ready today from platform targets whose verification gates are still open:\n\n| Target | Current status | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Web (SSG + Wasm SPA)** | Production-ready (`● proved` / `◐ trusted`) | Pre-rendered static HTML hydrating into a single `wasm32` binary (`app_lp2.wasm`) over the `LP2` v4 ABI. |\n| **Linux Desktop (GTK4 / PangoCairo)** | Experimental (`○ unknown` open gates) | Native C11 host rendering `.lpcss` flex/grid trees; full cross-platform UI gate still open. |\n| **Android, iOS, macOS, Windows** | Experimental (`○ unknown` open gates) | JNI Java, Swift UIKit/AppKit, and Win32 Direct2D adapters exist in `packages/`, with bounded runtime probes rather than closed UI gates. |\n\n<Callout kind=\"note\" title=\"Four-state evidence discipline\">\nThroughout Lakeport and this manual, every technical claim is labeled as **proved** (checked by the Lean 4.35 kernel with `0 sorryAx`), **trusted** (compiler, browser DOM, OS), **unknown** (open platform gates or live network conditions), or **failed** (a broken draft rejected by `lakeport check`).\n</Callout>\n\n## How this manual is organized\n\n1. **Getting started:** Install the CLI, connect `lakeport mcp`, and build a verified local-first outbox step by step.\n2. **Recommended patterns:** Structure pure client state, decode evolving backend JSON schemas without Lean on the server, and ship frontend-first releases across Service Worker caches.\n3. **Modules and capabilities:** Discover built-in SDKs (`lakeport-style`, `lakeport-router`, `lakeport-effects`, `lakeport-json`, `lakeport-xml`), capability plugins, CSS modules, routing, and dead-code stripping.\n4. **How Lakeport is built:** Inspect architecture diagrams, the `.lp` compiler and `LP2` runtime internals, and a candid code-quality guide for contributors.\n\nContinue to [Installation and setup](/docs/installation) to check your local toolchain.\n"
  | .installation => "---\ntitle: \"Installation and project layout\"\nsummary: \"Install the Lakeport CLI, verify compilers with lakeport doctor, wire lakeport mcp to your coding agent, and tour a clean src/ workspace.\"\nsection: \"Getting started\"\norder: 2\nreadingMinutes: 5\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Installation and project layout\n\nLakeport compiles `.lp` views, `.css` stylesheets, and Lean 4 modules into static HTML and WebAssembly through one CLI binary: `lakeport`.\n\n## 1. Verify your toolchain with lakeport doctor\n\nAfter placing `lakeport` on your `PATH`, run `lakeport doctor` to inspect the local tools used for type-checking and WebAssembly builds:\n\n```sh title=\"Check local build dependencies\"\nlakeport doctor\n```\n\nFor web development and production SSG builds, `lakeport doctor` checks:\n\n- **Lean 4.35 (`elan` / `lean` / `lake`):** Type-checks reducers, `.lp` views, and `*Laws.lean` theorems, and emits C11 sources.\n- **Emscripten (`emcc`) & Binaryen (`wasm-opt`):** Links C11 runtime code with `-Oz -flto -Wl,--gc-sections` into compact `wasm32` binaries.\n- **Node.js (v20+):** Executes `app_lp2.wasm` at build time to pre-render static HTML pages for every route in `ssgRoutes`.\n\nIf you only run `lakeport check` in CI or inside an agent loop, Emscripten is not invoked until `lakeport build web`.\n\n## 2. Connect your coding agent with lakeport mcp\n\nMost teams use Lakeport alongside Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor. Registering `lakeport mcp` gives your agent structured compiler diagnostics (exact `.lp`, `.css`, and `.lean` file paths, 1-indexed lines, error codes, and open proof goals) instead of raw terminal text:\n\n```toml title=\"Example MCP configuration\"\n[mcp_servers.lakeport]\ncommand = \"lakeport\"\nargs = [\"mcp\"]\n```\n\nOnce connected, your agent can iterate on a reducer and its `*Laws.lean` proof automatically before handing the result back to you.\n\n## 3. Standard single-app directory layout\n\nA Lakeport web app needs only `lakefile.toml` and `src/`. You do **not** need a `shared/` workspace package unless you are publishing a reusable library across multiple Lean repositories:\n\n```text title=\"Typical Lakeport web repository\"\nmy-app/\n  lakefile.toml          # App manifest, CSS entry, and ssgRoutes list\n  src/\n    app.css              # Global design tokens and component rules\n    App.lp               # Root StyledApp mount, navigation chrome, and views\n    Outbox.lean          # Pure client state and step reducer\n    OutboxLaws.lean      # [proof-only, 0 B C] theorems checked on every build\n    Api/\n      Orders.lean        # Frontend JSON decoders for your Go/Node/Python API\n```\n\n<Callout kind=\"tip\" title=\"Five SDKs are available automatically\">\nLakeport's build driver automatically imports `LakeportStyle`, `LakeportRouter`, `LakeportEffects`, `LakeportJson`, and `LakeportXml` into your project build graph, so you can open `LakeportJson` or `LakeportEffects` in any `.lean` or `.lp` module without extra `lakefile.toml` boilerplate.\n</Callout>\n\nNext, open [Your first verified app](/docs/getting-started) to build a local-first note outbox, trigger a real Lean proof failure on a buggy retry, and fix it.\n"
  | .gettingStarted => "---\ntitle: \"Your first verified app\"\nsummary: \"Build a local-first sync outbox step by step, watch lakeport check catch a duplicate-sync retry bug, fix the reducer, and wire a .lp view.\"\nsection: \"Getting started\"\norder: 3\nreadingMinutes: 10\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Your first verified app\n\nInstead of pasting a finished template, let us build a small **local-first note outbox** step by step so you can see how Lakeport's pieces connect and how a Lean proof actually catches a bug in client logic.\n\n## How a Lakeport app fits together\n\nEvery Lakeport screen follows a four-part loop:\n\n1. **`State` (`structure`):** Plain immutable data held on the client (for example, offline notes waiting to sync and notes confirmed by the server).\n2. **`Action` (`inductive`):** Every user click, text edit, or network callback that can happen.\n3. **`step : State → Action → State`:** Pure client business logic updating state (and optionally returning `EffectReq` commands like `httpPost` or `kvSet`).\n4. **`view : State → StyledView` (`.lp`):** JSX-style markup rendering `State` and mapping buttons or inputs back to `Action` values.\n\nWhen you add a `*Laws.lean` file, Lean checks properties of `step` across **every** possible `State` and `Action` at compile time, then erases the proof file to `0` bytes before compiling WebAssembly.\n\n## Step 1: Create the project manifest\n\nCreate a new directory `note-outbox/` with `lakefile.toml`:\n\n```toml title=\"lakefile.toml\"\nname = \"note-outbox\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndefaultTargets = [\"NoteOutbox\"]\n\n[[lean_lib]]\nname = \"NoteOutbox\"\nsrcDir = \"src\"\nroots = [\"App\", \"OutboxLaws\"]\n\n[lakeport]\nbundleId = \"dev.example.outbox\"\napplicationId = \"dev.example.outbox\"\nentry = \"App\"\napp = \"App.app\"\ncss = \"src/app.css\"\noutDir = \"dist\"\n\n[lakeport.web]\ntitle = \"Local-First Note Outbox\"\nmode = \"ssg\"\nssgRoutes = [\"/\"]\n```\n\nNotice `roots = [\"App\", \"OutboxLaws\"]`: `App` is your runtime entry point, while `OutboxLaws` is a leaf proof module that `lakeport check` verifies on every build and strips from C/Wasm codegen.\n\n## Step 2: Write a first draft reducer with a subtle retry bug\n\nIn a local-first app, users write notes offline or on spotty connections. Suppose we track three counts in `src/Outbox.lean`: `queued` notes on the device, `inFlight` (whether a note is currently being posted), and `synced` notes confirmed by the server.\n\nHere is a realistic first draft (similar to what a coding agent often writes on its first try) where a network timeout puts the in-flight note back into `queued` so the user can retry:\n\n```lean title=\"src/Outbox.lean (first draft with a retry bug)\"\nnamespace Outbox\n\ninductive Flight where\n  | idle\n  | sending\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\nstructure Model where\n  queued : Nat := 0\n  flight : Flight := .idle\n  synced : Nat := 0\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\ndef Model.totalNotes (m : Model) : Nat :=\n  m.queued + (if m.flight == .sending then 1 else 0) + m.synced\n\ninductive Action where\n  | addNote\n  | beginSync\n  | serverAck\n  | timeoutDrop\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ndef step (m : Model) : Action → Model\n  | .addNote =>\n    { m with queued := m.queued + 1 }\n  | .beginSync =>\n    if m.flight == .idle && m.queued > 0 then\n      { m with queued := m.queued - 1, flight := .sending }\n    else m\n  | .serverAck =>\n    /- Bug: late HTTP 200 arrives after timeoutDrop already re-queued the note! -/\n    { m with flight := .idle, synced := m.synced + 1 }\n  | .timeoutDrop =>\n    if m.flight == .sending then\n      { m with queued := m.queued + 1, flight := .idle }\n    else m\n\nend Outbox\n```\n\nCan you spot the bug? Happy-path tests (`addNote -> beginSync -> serverAck`) pass. Yet if a slow mobile connection triggers `timeoutDrop` (moving the note back to `queued`) and the original HTTP request still reaches the server and delivers a late `serverAck`, `synced` increments anyway and duplicates the user's note!\n\n## Step 3: State the conservation rule in OutboxLaws.lean\n\nAsk your coding agent (or write `src/OutboxLaws.lean` directly) to enforce a plain-English rule: **sync events (`beginSync`, `serverAck`, `timeoutDrop`) must never invent or lose notes; `totalNotes` may only change on `addNote`.**\n\n```lean title=\"src/OutboxLaws.lean\"\nimport Outbox\n\nnamespace Outbox.Laws\nopen Outbox\n\ntheorem sync_actions_conserve_notes (m : Model) (a : Action)\n    (hSync : a ≠ .addNote) :\n    (step m a).totalNotes = m.totalNotes := by\n  cases a with\n  | addNote => contradiction\n  | beginSync =>\n    dsimp [step, Model.totalNotes]\n    split <;> omega\n  | serverAck =>\n    dsimp [step, Model.totalNotes]\n    cases m.flight <;> omega\n  | timeoutDrop =>\n    dsimp [step, Model.totalNotes]\n    split <;> omega\n\nend Outbox.Laws\n```\n\nRun `lakeport check`:\n\n```sh title=\"Compiler catches the late-ack duplication bug\"\n$ lakeport check\nsrc/OutboxLaws.lean:16:22: error[lean]: omega could not prove the goal:\n  m : Model\n  hFlight : m.flight = Flight.idle\n  ⊢ m.queued + 0 + (m.synced + 1) = m.queued + 0 + m.synced\n```\n\nLook at that goal: Lean pinpointed the exact state (`m.flight = Flight.idle`) where `serverAck` added `1` to `m.synced` without a matching in-flight note!\n\n## Step 4: Fix the state machine and re-run lakeport check\n\nInstead of pretending a timed-out request never left the device, keep timed-out notes in `.awaitingConfirm` (with an idempotency key in a full app) and only accept `serverAck` when a note is actually `.sending` or `.awaitingConfirm`:\n\n```lean title=\"src/Outbox.lean (verified state machine)\"\nnamespace Outbox\n\ninductive Flight where\n  | idle\n  | sending\n  | awaitingConfirm\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\ndef Flight.count : Flight → Nat\n  | .idle => 0\n  | .sending => 1\n  | .awaitingConfirm => 1\n\nstructure Model where\n  queued : Nat := 0\n  flight : Flight := .idle\n  synced : Nat := 0\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\ndef Model.totalNotes (m : Model) : Nat :=\n  m.queued + m.flight.count + m.synced\n\ninductive Action where\n  | addNote\n  | beginSync\n  | serverAck\n  | timeoutDrop\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ndef step (m : Model) : Action → Model\n  | .addNote =>\n    { m with queued := m.queued + 1 }\n  | .beginSync =>\n    if m.flight == .idle && m.queued > 0 then\n      { m with queued := m.queued - 1, flight := .sending }\n    else m\n  | .serverAck =>\n    match m.flight with\n    | .sending | .awaitingConfirm =>\n      { m with flight := .idle, synced := m.synced + 1 }\n    | .idle => m\n  | .timeoutDrop =>\n    match m.flight with\n    | .sending => { m with flight := .awaitingConfirm }\n    | _ => m\n\nend Outbox\n```\n\nUpdate the `beginSync` / `serverAck` / `timeoutDrop` branches in `OutboxLaws.lean` with `cases h : m.flight <;> simp_all [Flight.count] <;> omega` and run `lakeport check` again:\n\n```sh title=\"Zero-byte proof verification passes\"\n$ lakeport check\n  check Outbox\n  check OutboxLaws [proof-only, 0 B C]\nok (0 sorryAx, 0 unused declarations)\n```\n\n## Step 5: Render the UI in src/App.lp and src/app.css\n\nNow connect `Outbox.Model` and `Outbox.step` to JSX markup in `src/App.lp` and styles in `src/app.css`:\n\n```css title=\"src/app.css\"\n.shell { max-width: 560px; margin: 48px auto; padding: 24px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px; }\n.title { font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; }\n.stats { display: flex; gap: 16px; font-family: monospace; }\n.controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; }\n.btn { padding: 8px 16px; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #BDBEBC; background: #FFFFFF; cursor: pointer; }\n```\n\n```lp title=\"src/App.lp\"\nimport Outbox\n\nnamespace App\nopen LakeportStyle Outbox\n\ndef encodeAction : Action → Nat\n  | .addNote => 1\n  | .beginSync => 2\n  | .serverAck => 3\n  | .timeoutDrop => 4\n\ndef decodeAction : Nat → Option Action\n  | 1 => some .addNote\n  | 2 => some .beginSync\n  | 3 => some .serverAck\n  | 4 => some .timeoutDrop\n  | _ => none\n\ndef view (m : Model) : StyledView Action Nat :=\n  <main class=\"shell\">\n    <h1 class=\"title\">Local-First Note Outbox</h1>\n    <div class=\"stats\">\n      <span>{\"Queued: \" ++ toString m.queued}</span>\n      <span>{\"In flight: \" ++ toString m.flight.count}</span>\n      <span>{\"Synced: \" ++ toString m.synced}</span>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"controls\">\n      <button class=\"btn\" onClick={Action.addNote}>Write offline note</button>\n      <button class=\"btn\" onClick={Action.beginSync}>Send next note</button>\n      <button class=\"btn\" onClick={Action.timeoutDrop}>Simulate Wi-Fi drop</button>\n      <button class=\"btn\" onClick={Action.serverAck}>Deliver server 200 OK</button>\n    </div>\n  </main>\n\ndef app : StyledApp Model Unit Action Nat where\n  initial := fun _ => {}\n  view := view\n  encodeEvent := fun a => .nat (encodeAction a)\n  decodeEvent := fun | .nat n => decodeAction n | _ => none\n  encodeField := .nat\n  decodeField := fun | .nat n => some n | _ => none\n  press := fun m a => (step m a, [])\n  edit := fun m _ _ => some (m, [])\n  message := fun m _ => (m, [])\n  decodeNamed := fun _ _ => .error (.codec .badTag)\n  commandImage := fun _ => none\n  appCommit := fun m => m.totalNotes\n  descriptor := \"note-outbox/1\"\n  profile := ⟨⟨32, 65536⟩, ⟨32, 65536⟩⟩\n\nend App\n```\n\nRun `lakeport dev` to test in your browser, or `lakeport build web --release` to emit pre-rendered HTML and `app_lp2.wasm` in `dist/web/`.\n\n<Callout kind=\"important\" title=\"A candid note on <button>, clickable cards, and gestures\">\nAs soon as you build richer UI beyond text buttons (such as a clickable card with an icon and badge), you will notice that Lakeport currently only permits `onClick` on `<button>`, and `<button>` only accepts a `String` label. That is **not** a philosophical feature; it is a real constraint inherited from freezing Lakeport's 4-constructor `ViewApi` kernel early (and it is the exact reason React Native provides `Pressable` / `onPress` on arbitrary containers). In [Views, SDKs, and .lp TSX](/docs/views-and-tsx), we show the `.hit-overlay` CSS workaround used across `lakeport.dev` today and state plainly which gestures (such as touch swiping) are not yet wired into Lean.\n</Callout>\n\nNext, continue to **Recommended patterns** starting with [Client state and proofs](/docs/pure-core-and-proofs).\n"
  | .pureCoreAndProofs => "---\ntitle: \"Client state and plain-English proofs\"\nsummary: \"Model local-first business logic in pure Lean reducers, avoid vacuous theorems with productive witnesses, and erase proofs to zero bytes.\"\nsection: \"Recommended patterns\"\norder: 4\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Client state and plain-English proofs\n\nYou do not get value from Lean by proving theorems about CSS padding or header links. Lakeport works best when you keep visual `.lp` templates lightweight and isolate stateful client business logic (local-first sync queues, optimistic mutations, undo/redo stacks, checkout locks, and schema decoders) inside pure `.lean` modules.\n\n## Separate domain transitions from host I/O\n\nIn a typical TypeScript frontend, event handlers interleave state mutation with `await fetch(...)`, timers, and `localStorage` writes. Testing every race condition requires mocking half the browser.\n\nIn Lakeport, your domain reducer is a pure function:\n\n```lean title=\"Pure local-first reducer signature\"\ndef step (state : SyncState) (action : SyncAction) : SyncState × List EffectReq\n```\n\nGiven any `SyncState` and `SyncAction`, `step` computes the next `SyncState` and a plain data list of outbound `EffectReq` values (such as `.httpPost`, `.kvSet`, or `.sleepMs`). Because there are no hidden globals, `Float` rounding drift, or async callbacks inside `step`, Lean can reason directly about every reachable branch.\n\n## Prompting your coding agent for meaningful rules\n\nWhen working with Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor over `lakeport mcp`, state invariants in plain domain terms rather than Lean jargon:\n\n- *\"Prove that retrying a timed-out payment never decreases available + held + settled cents, and never emits a second `POST /charge` while an earlier charge awaits confirmation.\"*\n- *\"Prove that applying optimistic local edits and out-of-order server acknowledgements never drops an unacknowledged user edit from the outbox.\"*\n- *\"Prove that clicking Back from any modal sheet or nested page strictly decreases navigation depth until reaching the root screen.\"*\n\n## Always pair safety theorems with a productive witness\n\nOne classic trap when asking an AI coding agent for a safety proof is **vacuous satisfaction**: an agent can make *\"never overcharges\"* trivially true by breaking checkout so nobody is ever charged at all!\n\nPair every safety invariant (`never_overcharges`) with at least one **productive witness** theorem showing the happy path actually completes work:\n\n```lean title=\"Safety invariant + productive witness in *Laws.lean\"\n/- 1. Safety across all network runs (including mid-flight disconnects) -/\ntheorem lakeportFixed_never_overcharges (net : NetworkRun) :\n    (runCheckout .lakeportFixed net).chargedCents ≤ 2500 := by\n  cases net <;> decide\n\n/- 2. Productive witness: a normal run actually settles $25.00 -/\ntheorem lakeportFixed_settles_normal_wifi :\n    (runCheckout .lakeportFixed .normalWifi).chargedCents = 2500 := by\n  decide\n```\n\nWith both theorems in `WorkbenchLaws.lean`, an agent cannot cheat either side: breaking the happy path fails `lakeportFixed_settles_normal_wifi`, and breaking the retry lock fails `lakeportFixed_never_overcharges`.\n\n## Leaf law modules compile to 0 runtime bytes\n\nKeep your `theorem` declarations in dedicated `*Laws.lean` leaf modules listed in `lakefile.toml` `roots` (imported by nothing in `App.lp`):\n\n1. `lakeport check` elaborates every `*Laws.lean` module through the Lean 4.35 kernel (`0 sorryAx`).\n2. During `lakeport build`, Lakeport detects that `*Laws.lean` contains zero runtime code reachable from `App.app`, logs `[proof-only, 0 B C]`, and skips C and WebAssembly code generation for those files completely.\n\n## What proofs establish (and what they never establish)\n\nA kernel-checked proof guarantees that your compiled pure Lean model satisfies the stated mathematical property (assuming the Lean 4.35 kernel and C/Wasm compiler). It does **not** prove:\n\n- That a third-party REST server honors its documented contract.\n- That `localStorage` will not be cleared by the user's browser.\n- That your `.css` layout looks right on a small viewport.\n\nUse proofs for high-consequence client state machines, and combine them with runtime schema validation at the HTTP boundary, covered next in [Frontend JSON schema unions](/docs/versioned-contracts).\n"
  | .versionedContracts => "---\ntitle: \"Frontend JSON schema unions\"\nsummary: \"Decode active v1 and v2 JSON payloads from Go, Node, Python, or Rust servers right in your frontend, and use headers when gating writes.\"\nsection: \"Recommended patterns\"\norder: 5\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Frontend JSON schema unions\n\nAlmost nobody using Lakeport runs Lean on their backend. Your API is far more likely written in Go, Node/TypeScript, Python, Rust, or Rails, and almost every endpoint speaks **JSON**.\n\nMeanwhile, you do not control when every client upgrades:\n\n- Users leave browser tabs open for days across sleep/wake cycles.\n- **Service Workers** serve precached or `stale-while-revalidate` WebAssembly and JS bundles until the next navigation or cache rotation.\n- Local-first outboxes replay queued edits created before your latest server deploy.\n\nYou do not need a shared client/server Lean monorepo to keep those clients working. Instead, model the **active backend JSON schemas** as an explicit union right inside your frontend's `src/Api/` folder.\n\n## 1. Decode active backend JSON schemas in src/Api/\n\nSuppose your backend team is migrating `GET /api/orders/:id` from a flat `v1` JSON object (`total_cents`, `status`) to a nested `v2` JSON object (`amount.cents`, `amount.currency`, `refundable`).\n\nInstead of overwriting your old decoder in place, define an inductive union of the schemas currently alive in production using Lakeport's built-in `LakeportJson` SDK:\n\n```lean title=\"src/Api/Orders.lean (frontend-only JSON schema union)\"\nimport LakeportJson.Codec\nopen LakeportJson\n\nstructure OrderV1 where\n  id : String\n  totalCents : Nat\n  status : String\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\nstructure OrderV2 where\n  id : String\n  cents : Nat\n  currency : String\n  refundable : Bool\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ninductive OrderSchema where\n  | v1 (order : OrderV1)\n  | v2 (order : OrderV2)\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ndef decodeOrderSchema (rawJson : String) : Option OrderSchema :=\n  match Json.parse rawJson with\n  | .error _ => none\n  | .ok j =>\n    match (do\n      let id ← j.fieldAs String \"id\"\n      let amt ← j.field \"amount\"\n      let cents ← amt.fieldAs Nat \"cents\"\n      let currency ← amt.fieldAs String \"currency\"\n      let refundable ← j.fieldAs Bool \"refundable\"\n      pure (OrderSchema.v2 { id, cents, currency, refundable })) with\n    | .ok v2Order => some v2Order\n    | .error _ =>\n      match (do\n        let id ← j.fieldAs String \"id\"\n        let totalCents ← j.fieldAs Nat \"total_cents\"\n        let status ← j.fieldAs String \"status\"\n        pure (OrderSchema.v1 { id, totalCents, status })) with\n      | .ok v1Order => some v1Order\n      | .error _ => none\n```\n\nIn your reducer and `.lp` views, match exhaustively on `OrderSchema` **without a wildcard `_ =>` arm**:\n\n```lean title=\"Exhaustive handling in your frontend\"\ndef formatOrderSummary : OrderSchema → String\n  | .v1 o => \"Order \" ++ o.id ++ \": $\" ++ toString (o.totalCents / 100) ++ \" (\" ++ o.status ++ \")\"\n  | .v2 o => \"Order \" ++ o.id ++ \": $\" ++ toString (o.cents / 100) ++ \" \" ++ o.currency\n```\n\n## 2. Do backends need to advertise their version?\n\nBe pragmatic about version advertisement:\n\n- **For reading responses, schema compliance is already proof of the version.** If `GET /api/orders/ord-42` parses as `OrderSchema.v1` or `OrderSchema.v2`, your frontend already knows which schema arrived without calling a separate `/api/capabilities` endpoint.\n- **For gating new write actions, advertising capabilities is a helpful recommendation.** Before a user clicks a brand-new `Request instant refund` button (`POST /api/orders/:id/refund`), your UI wants to know whether the backend fleet actually supports that endpoint yet.\n- **HTTP response headers are a lightweight convenience.** Having your Go, Node, or Python server include headers such as `X-API-Version: 2` or `X-Capabilities: instant-refund` (read via `EffectReq.httpRequest` / `EffectResp.httpResponse` headers, or carried as a boolean in the JSON body) lets the frontend hide or disable `v2`-only write buttons until your backend rollout is complete.\n\n## 3. Interactive frontend JSON & header simulator\n\nClick through the four `GET /api/orders/ord-42` server responses below. Each button feeds the displayed raw JSON string through `LakeportJson.Json.parse` and `decodeOrderSchema` inside this page's WebAssembly binary:\n\n<ContractSimulator />\n\n## 4. The four-step frontend-first lifecycle\n\n1. **Add the new schema to `src/Api/`:** Keep `OrderSchema.v1` and add `OrderSchema.v2`. Verify with `lakeport check` that every screen handles both branches.\n2. **Release the frontend first:** Deploy your static assets / Cloudflare Worker. Clients now accept `v1` and `v2` JSON while your backend still serves `v1`.\n3. **Roll out your backend:** Deploy the updated server returning `v2` JSON (and optionally advertising `X-Capabilities` once every server replica is upgraded).\n4. **Delete `OrderSchema.v1` when retired:** Once backend logs show zero `v1` traffic and Service Worker caches have rotated, delete the `| v1` constructor. `lakeport check` points to every dead `v1` branch so you can clean it up in one pass.\n\nNext, read [Frontend-first rollouts and Service Workers](/docs/frontend-first-releases) to see how rolling deploys, cached bundles, and offline outboxes interact during a live release.\n"
  | .frontendFirstReleases => "---\ntitle: \"Frontend-first rollouts and caching\"\nsummary: \"Coordinate rolling backend deploys, long-lived browser tabs, Service Worker caches, and reducer-level write gates without downtime.\"\nsection: \"Recommended patterns\"\norder: 6\nreadingMinutes: 7\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Frontend-first rollouts and caching\n\nTeams often assume that deploying their frontend and backend from the same CI pipeline means users run matching versions. On the web (and even more so on mobile or desktop), client and server deploys never switch over atomically:\n\n- **Rolling backend deploys:** Load balancers route consecutive requests from the same browser session to old and new server pods while containers drain.\n- **Open browser tabs:** Users keep productivity apps, dashboards, and checkouts open for days without reloading `index.html`.\n- **Service Worker caching:** Offline-capable web apps precache `app_lp2.wasm` and `bundle.css` (or serve HTML with `stale-while-revalidate`), meaning returning users boot the previously cached client bundle at least once before an updated Service Worker activates.\n- **Local-first offline outboxes:** Edits queued before lunch may replay hours later against a newly deployed backend.\n\n## Interactive rollout timeline\n\nStep through the four phases below to trace what your backend returns, what a Service Worker cached tab sees, and when new `v2` write actions open up:\n\n<RolloutTimeline />\n\n## Gate new write commands inside your reducer as well as JSX\n\nHiding a button in a `.lp` view (`if s.canInstantRefund then <button ...>`) improves UX, yet a hidden button alone does not stop:\n\n- Keyboard shortcuts (`data-hotkey`) firing the same action constructor.\n- Queued local-first outbox commands replaying after a feature flag is turned back off during an incident.\n- Agent-written refactorings adding a second button calling the same event tag.\n\nCheck server capability state directly inside your pure reducer before emitting the `EffectReq`:\n\n```lean title=\"Reducer-level write gate in src/State.lean\"\ndef requestInstantRefund (s : AppState) (orderId : String) : AppState × List EffectReq :=\n  if s.serverCapabilities.contains \"instant-refund\" then\n    let reqId := s.nextReqId\n    let url := \"/api/orders/\" ++ orderId ++ \"/refund\"\n    ({ s with nextReqId := reqId + 1, refundInFlight := true },\n     [.httpPost reqId url \"application/json\" \"{}\"])\n  else\n    ({ s with notice := some \"Instant refund is not enabled on the server yet.\" }, [])\n```\n\nWith that check in `requestInstantRefund`, flipping `X-Capabilities` (or a feature flag field) off on your backend during a rollback cleanly halts new `v2` writes without requiring an emergency frontend redeploy or Service Worker cache purge.\n\n## Pair Service Worker updates with versioned Wasm URLs\n\nWhen configuring a Service Worker or edge CDN (such as Cloudflare Workers static assets):\n\n1. Serve `index.html` and route SSG HTML pages with `Cache-Control: no-cache` (or short `stale-while-revalidate`) so fresh navigations discover new asset URLs promptly.\n2. Cache immutable font files (`.woff2`) aggressively, and version or revalidate `/wasm/app_lp2.wasm` + `/runtime/boot.mjs` together so `boot.mjs` and `app_lp2.wasm` never mismatch across `LP2` ABI versions.\n3. Keep retired backend JSON schemas in `src/Api/` until your Service Worker max-age / activation window has elapsed.\n\nNext, explore **Modules and capabilities** starting with [SDK map, views, and .lp TSX](/docs/views-and-tsx).\n"
  | .viewsAndTsx => "---\ntitle: \"SDK map, views, and .lp TSX\"\nsummary: \"Discover Lakeport's built-in SDKs and capability plugins, write typed .lp JSX views, and understand current button and gesture limits.\"\nsection: \"Modules and capabilities\"\norder: 7\nreadingMinutes: 9\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# SDK map, views, and .lp TSX\n\nBefore diving into `.lp` component syntax, here is a complete map of the **modules and capabilities** bundled with Lakeport so you know what exists out of the box without hunting through source directories.\n\n## Built-in SDKs and capability plugins\n\nLakeport automatically wires **five pure-Lean SDKs** into every application build, plus **three optional capability plugins** for streaming transports, device hardware, and local inference:\n\n| Module | Import / ID | What it gives you |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `lakeport-style` | `open LakeportStyle` | `StyledView` JSX nodes, CSS class verification, `MemoCell` / `MemoKeyedList`, and binary `LPST` style diffing. |\n| `lakeport-router` | `open LakeportRouter` | `UrlCodec`, `LawfulUrlCodec` round-trip proofs, `RouterState` (stack, sheet, drawer), and `<RouterShell>`. |\n| `lakeport-effects` | `open LakeportEffects` | `18` typed `EffectReq` / `EffectResp` host commands (HTTP, WS, KV, clipboard, haptics, timers, plugins) and idempotent `Outbox`. |\n| `lakeport-json` | `open LakeportJson` | SWAR-accelerated RFC 8259 `Json.parse`, `Json.stringify`, `ToJson` / `FromJson`, `fieldAs`, streaming NDJSON, and round-trip laws. |\n| `lakeport-xml` | `open LakeportXml` | Pure-Lean XML lexer, tree parser, serializer, and escapes for RSS, SVG, and enterprise XML payloads. |\n| `@lakeport/transport` | Plugin ID `\"transport\"` | Server-Sent Events (SSE), chunked binary frame streams, and multipart uploads beyond the core `64 KB` effect ceiling. |\n| `@lakeport/media-device` | Plugin ID `\"media-device\"` | Camera/microphone capture, geolocation, Bluetooth LE scans, network reachability, and native haptics. |\n| `@lakeport/on-device-ai` | Plugin ID `\"on-device-ai\"` | Structured invocation and token streaming for local/on-device model runtimes. |\n\n## Supported .lp JSX elements\n\nLakeport `.lp` files let you write JSX markup directly inside Lean 4 modules. Prior to elaboration, `LpTsx.lean` lowers JSX tags to `LakeportStyle.StyledView` values while preserving 1:1 line numbers so compiler diagnostics point straight at your `.lp` file:\n\n- **Layout and landmarks (`10`):** `div`, `span`, `header`, `section`, `main`, `aside`, `footer`, `nav`, `p`, and headings `h1`, `h2`, `h3` (`12` text/container tags total)\n- **Interactive controls (`3`):** `button`, `input`, `textarea`\n- **Media and vector graphics:** `img` (requiring `src` and `alt`) and `svg` (`path`, `circle`, `rect`, `line`, `polyline`, `polygon`, `ellipse`, `g`, `defs`, `linearGradient`, `clipPath`, lowered to `data-lp-svg*` attributes for Web DOM and native vector renderers)\n\n## Candid guide: buttons, .hit-overlay, and gestures (like swiping)\n\nIf you try writing `<div onClick={ev}>` or nesting an `<svg>` icon and two `<span>`s inside `<button>`, `lakeport check` rejects it:\n\n1. `onClick={ev}` is only accepted on `<button>`.\n2. `<button>` only accepts a `String` child (no nested elements).\n\n**Why is it like this?** Honestly, this is an architectural trade-off (and a rough edge) from freezing Lakeport's verified kernel (`ViewApi` in `lakeport-core`) early around four minimal constructors: `.text`, `.button (label : String) (action : E)`, `.input`, and `.group`. Keeping `ViewApi` tiny made it tractable to prove reconciliation and capacity rollback theorems, yet in real UI work people constantly need clickable cards, icon buttons, and row items. It is the exact reason React Native introduced `Pressable` and `onPress` on non-button containers rather than forcing every tap target through a text-only button.\n\n**How to build clickable cards and icon buttons today (`.hit-overlay`):** Layer a full-bleed `<button class=\"hit-overlay\" onClick={ev}>Accessible label</button>` inside a `position: relative` container and set `pointer-events: none` on visual sibling elements:\n\n```lp title=\"Clickable card workaround used across lakeport.dev\"\ndef chapterCard (title summary : String) (ev : UiEvent) : V :=\n  <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n    <button class=\"hit-overlay\" onClick={ev}>{title}</button>\n    <span class=\"chapter-title\">{title}</span>\n    <p class=\"chapter-summary\">{summary}</p>\n  </div>\n```\n\n```css title=\"src/app.css\"\n.chapter-card { position: relative; }\n.hit-overlay { position: absolute; inset: 0; opacity: 0; cursor: pointer; }\n.chapter-title, .chapter-summary { pointer-events: none; }\n```\n\nThis keeps real `<button>` keyboard focus, Enter/Space activation, and screen-reader labels intact, though it is undeniably boilerplate compared to a native `Pressable` container.\n\n**What about swiping and touch gestures?** Today, Lakeport's core event channel models discrete **presses** (`onClick`), **controlled text edits** (`field={id}`), **keyboard shortcuts** (`data-hotkey`, `data-submit`), and **pointer ghost-drag reorder/drop** (`data-lp-draggable` / `data-lp-drop-zone` mapped onto synthetic buttons). Continuous touch gestures (**horizontal swipe-to-dismiss, pan/pinch, scroll position callbacks, and focus/blur events**) do **not** cross into Lean state yet. On the web, CSS scroll-snap carousels work purely in the browser, while gesture-driven state machines require extending `ViewApi` or bridging a host gesture plugin.\n\n## Controlled inputs and keyed lists\n\n`<input>` and `<textarea>` bind to your `StyledApp`'s field identifier type `F` via `field={...}`:\n\n```lp title=\"Controlled search box\"\n<input\n  class=\"search-input\"\n  field={1}\n  value={state.query}\n  label=\"Filter chapters\"\n  placeholder=\"Filter chapters...\"\n/>\n```\n\nWhen rendering dynamic `List` or `Array` collections that can reorder or delete mid-list, supply a stable `Nat` key with `key={item.id}` (or use `MemoKeyedList` for large lists).\n\n## Zero-stale-dependency caching with MemoCell\n\nUnlike React's `useMemo`, where forgetting an item in `[depA, depB]` causes stale UI bugs, `LakeportStyle.MemoCell α β f` stores `(input : α)`, `(output : β)`, and an erased proof `coherent : output = f input`.\n\nOn a cache hit (`c.input == x`), `c.refresh x` returns the cached `output` with zero allocations; on a change, it recomputes `f x`. The kernel theorem `MemoCell.refresh_get` guarantees `(c.refresh x).get = f x`, and `coherent` erases to `0` bytes at runtime.\n\nNext, read [Styling, CSS modules, and .lpcss](/docs/styling-and-lpcss).\n"
  | .stylingAndLpcss => "---\ntitle: \"Styling, CSS modules, and .lpcss\"\nsummary: \"Catch CSS class typos at compile time, scope package styles with import_css, drive dynamic state via data-*, and emit .lpcss for native shells.\"\nsection: \"Modules and capabilities\"\norder: 8\nreadingMinutes: 6\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Styling, CSS modules, and .lpcss\n\nStylesheets in Lakeport are ordinary `.css` files verified at compile time rather than untyped runtime strings or JavaScript CSS-in-JS objects. One `.css` pipeline produces `bundle.css` for browsers and a binary `LPCS` (`bundle.lpcss`) style sheet for native host shells.\n\n## Compile-time class verification\n\nWhen you write `class=\"card card-header\"` in a `.lp` file, Lakeport parses `[lakeport] css = \"src/app.css\"` before Lean runs and checks every class token against your stylesheet and Lakeport's built-in layout utility index.\n\nRenaming `.card-header` in `src/app.css` without updating a `.lp` reference fails `lakeport check` immediately with a source-mapped error:\n\n```sh title=\"Compile-time CSS diagnostic\"\nsrc/Marketing.lp:42:10: error[css-global]: unknown CSS class \"card-header\":\n  not defined by the global stylesheet and not a supported utility\n```\n\n## Scoped package stylesheets with import_css\n\nReusable packages (such as `packages/lakeport-mdx`) should never collide with an application's global class names. Any `.lp` module can bind a co-located stylesheet using `import_css`:\n\n```lp title=\"packages/lakeport-mdx/src/LakeportMdx/Render.lp\"\nimport LakeportMdx.Ast\nimport_css MdxCss from \"./Mdx.css\"\n\ndef badge (lang : String) : StyledView E F :=\n  <span class={MdxCss.langBadge}>{lang}</span>\n```\n\nDuring lowering, Lakeport computes a 6-character SHA-256 prefix from the module identity, rewrites `.langBadge` into a scoped selector inside Cascade Layer L5 of `bundle.css`, and exposes `MdxCss.langBadge : ClassName` so typos fail compilation cleanly.\n\n## Dynamic state with data-* attributes and CSS custom properties\n\nStatic `class=\"...\"` strings allow the compiler to hoist `NodeStyle` records into top-level constants (`_lp_ns_k`) instead of allocating class arrays on every render frame. Express dynamic visual state with typed `data-*` attributes and `--*` CSS custom properties:\n\n```lp title=\"Static class + typed data attribute\"\n<div class=\"sync-pill\" data-status={state.statusSlug} --pill-progress={progressPct}>\n  <span>{state.statusLabel}</span>\n</div>\n```\n\n```css title=\"src/app.css\"\n.sync-pill {\n  padding: 4px 12px;\n  border-radius: 8px;\n  background: var(--surface);\n  color: var(--ink-muted);\n}\n.sync-pill[data-status=\"synced\"] {\n  background: var(--proved-soft);\n  color: var(--proved);\n}\n```\n\n## Light and dark themes via token overrides\n\nDefine semantic surface, text, border, and evidence variables (`--bg`, `--surface`, `--ink`, `--brand`, `--proved`, `--trusted`, `--unknown`, `--failed`) on your root shell class, then override **only the variables** under `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` and `[data-theme=\"dark\"]`. Every component inherits both themes automatically without duplicating layout rules.\n\nNext, read [Routing, LawfulUrlCodec, and SSG](/docs/routing-and-ssg) to see how URLs round-trip cleanly and pre-render to static HTML.\n"
  | .routingAndSsg => "---\ntitle: \"Routing, LawfulUrlCodec, and SSG\"\nsummary: \"Model URLs with an inductive Route sum and a LawfulUrlCodec round-trip proof, manage back stacks purely, and pre-render static HTML.\"\nsection: \"Modules and capabilities\"\norder: 9\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Routing, LawfulUrlCodec, and SSG\n\nWhile backend REST APIs speak standard JSON, URL path segments are one place where every app defines its own custom string parser and formatter. `LakeportRouter` backs your URL router with an inductive `Route` type and a small `LawfulUrlCodec` round-trip proof so internal links and pre-rendered SSG paths never drift out of sync.\n\n## 1. Define your Route type and LawfulUrlCodec\n\nList every screen in your application as an inductive type in `src/Routes.lean`:\n\n```lean title=\"src/Routes.lean\"\ninductive SiteRoute where\n  | home\n  | whyLean\n  | ecosystem\n  | showcase\n  | docsIndex\n  | docsPage (slug : DocSlug)\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n```\n\nImplement `UrlCodec SiteRoute` by converting between `SiteRoute` and path segments (`List String`), then prove `LawfulUrlCodec` in `src/SiteLaws.lean`:\n\n```lean title=\"Round-trip URL codec and theorem\"\ndef routeCodec : UrlCodec SiteRoute where\n  toSegments\n    | .home => []\n    | .whyLean => [\"why-lean\"]\n    | .ecosystem => [\"ecosystem\"]\n    | .showcase => [\"showcase\"]\n    | .docsIndex => [\"docs\"]\n    | .docsPage d => [\"docs\", d.slug]\n  fromSegments?\n    | [] => some .home\n    | [\"why-lean\"] => some .whyLean\n    | [\"ecosystem\"] => some .ecosystem\n    | [\"showcase\"] => some .showcase\n    | [\"docs\"] => some .docsIndex\n    | [\"docs\", raw] => DocSlug.parse? raw |>.map .docsPage\n    | _ => none\n\ninstance : LawfulUrlCodec routeCodec where\n  roundtrip r := by cases r <;> first | rfl | (rename_i d; cases d <;> rfl)\n```\n\nIf an agent adds a new `DocSlug` chapter or screen constructor and forgets `fromSegments?`, `lakeport check` stops immediately with the unhandled case.\n\n## 2. Pure navigation state and RouterShell\n\n`RouterState Route` models your root route, a push/pop back-stack, an optional modal sheet, and an optional side drawer as pure Lean values. Its companion theorem `RouterState.stepBack_decreases` proves that pressing Back closes open overlays first and pops the stack until reaching the root screen.\n\nWrap your top-level `.lp` view in `<RouterShell>` so `boot.mjs` synchronizes `window.history`:\n\n```lp title=\"Synchronizing browser history in src/App.lp\"\ndef view (s : AppState) : V :=\n  let url := routePath s.router.activeRoute\n  <RouterShell url={url} canBack={s.router.canGoBack}>\n    <div class=\"site-shell\" data-theme={s.theme.attr}>\n      {headerView s}\n      {routeBody s}\n      {footerView}\n    </div>\n  </RouterShell>\n```\n\nForward route transitions (`history.pushState`) reset scroll position to the top of the document, while browser Back/Forward (`popstate`) preserves native scroll restoration.\n\n## 3. Static site generation (SSG) from compiled WebAssembly\n\nWhen `[lakeport.web] mode = \"ssg\"` is configured in `lakefile.toml`, `lakeport build web --release` runs `ssg-prerender.mjs` in Node.js against your actual `app_lp2.wasm` binary for every URL in `ssgRoutes`:\n\n1. Node boots `app_lp2.wasm`, navigates to `/docs/versioned-contracts`, and serializes the resulting `ViewApi` + `LPST` frame into complete `dist/web/docs/versioned-contracts/index.html` with `data-lp-id` hydration attributes.\n2. In the browser, static HTML paints immediately before WebAssembly finishes downloading.\n3. When `boot.mjs` mounts on a matching `data-lp-ssg-route`, `DomRenderer.hydrate` binds to the existing DOM nodes in place without recreating elements or causing layout shift.\n\n<Callout kind=\"note\" title=\"Current URL parser limitation (?query and #hash)\">\n`LakeportRouter.Url.parseUrl` currently strips `?query` parameters and `#hash` fragments before passing path segments into `fromSegments?`. Apps that persist filter state in URLs should encode filter segments into the path (for example `/orders/status/open`) until structured query codecs land in `lakeport-router`.\n</Callout>\n\nNext, read [Effects, JSON, and offline sync](/docs/effects-and-offline).\n"
  | .effectsAndOffline => "---\ntitle: \"Effects, JSON, and offline sync\"\nsummary: \"Use Lakeport's 18 typed EffectReq commands, LakeportJson/Xml codecs, Service Worker caches, and idempotent offline outboxes.\"\nsection: \"Modules and capabilities\"\norder: 10\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Effects, JSON, and offline sync\n\nBecause your reducer is pure, side effects cross an explicit data boundary: `press`, `edit`, and `message` return `(State × List Term)` built from `LakeportEffects.EffectReq`. The host runtime executes each request asynchronously and feeds a typed `EffectResp` back into `app.message`.\n\n## All 18 built-in EffectReq operations\n\n`LakeportEffects` provides `18` typed host commands grouped into five families:\n\n- **HTTP & Streaming (`5`):** `.httpGet`, `.httpPost`, `.httpRequest` (custom method + request headers), `.httpStream` (chunked / SSE delivery), `.httpCancel`\n- **WebSockets (`4`):** `.wsConnect`, `.wsSend`, `.wsSendBytes`, `.wsClose`\n- **Key-Value Storage (`3`):** `.kvGet`, `.kvSet`, `.kvDelete` (`localStorage` on web; native preferences on desktop and mobile shells)\n- **UX & Timers (`3`):** `.clipboardWrite`, `.haptic` (`light`, `medium`, `heavy`, `success`, `warning`, `error`), `.sleepMs`\n- **Capability Plugins (`3`):** `.pluginInvoke`, `.pluginSubscribe`, `.pluginUnsubscribe` (used by `@lakeport/transport`, `@lakeport/media-device`, and `@lakeport/on-device-ai`)\n\nEvery command carries a caller-chosen `reqId : Nat` so your reducer can correlate responses, timeouts, and cancellations deterministically.\n\n## Parsing JSON and XML without Float drift\n\n`LakeportJson` and `LakeportXml` are pure Lean 4.35 SDKs with zero external C parser dependencies:\n\n- **`LakeportJson`:** Uses exact decimal `JsonNumber` (`mantissa : Int`, `exponent : Nat`) rather than IEEE-754 `Float`, so parsing stays bit-identical across `wasm32` and 64-bit native targets. Includes `Json.parse`, `Json.stringify`, `ToJson` / `FromJson`, `j.fieldAs`, `j.fieldOpt`, streaming `JsonChunkParser`, and parallel `JsonTape` indexing.\n- **`LakeportXml`:** Provides total fuel-bounded XML lexing, element tree parsing, and attribute/entity escaping.\n\n## Local-first Outbox and mid-flight disconnects\n\nLocal-first apps persist user edits locally first (via `.kvSet`) and sync them in the background. Network drops expose a critical distinction that naive retry loops get wrong:\n\n1. **Definite server rejection (HTTP `4xx` / `422`):** The server never applied the mutation; mark the item rejected or ask the user to edit it.\n2. **Uncertain delivery (`.unknownOutcome`: socket drop, airplane mode, `502`/`504` timeout):** Your request bytes *may or may not* have reached the backend database before the connection died.\n\n`LakeportEffects.Outbox` models each queued entry with a stable client-generated idempotency key (`clientMutationId`). When an HTTP call fails with a network drop, transition that entry to `.unknownOutcome` and replay the **same** `clientMutationId` on reconnect rather than minting a brand-new mutation ID.\n\n## Service Worker caching for offline Lakeport web apps\n\nTo make a Lakeport web app boot offline, register a standard browser Service Worker alongside your `dist/web/` output:\n\n- **Precache core runtime assets:** Cache `/wasm/app_lp2.wasm`, `/wasm/app_lp2.js`, `/runtime/boot.mjs`, `/bundle.css`, and your `.woff2` fonts inside one versioned cache key (`lakeport-shell-vN`) so the JS shim and `.wasm` binary always upgrade together.\n- **Route HTML strategy:** Use `network-first` (falling back to cached SSG HTML when offline) or `stale-while-revalidate` for navigation requests so online users pick up new deployments promptly while offline users still boot immediately.\n- **Keep backend schema unions aligned with cache lifetime:** Remember that a user booting from a Service Worker cache runs yesterday's `.wasm` binary against today's server until the new Service Worker activates; see [Frontend JSON schema unions](/docs/versioned-contracts).\n\n<Callout kind=\"warning\" title=\"64 KB ceiling on core EffectResp payloads\">\nBecause core `EffectResp` messages cross the fixed `64 KiB` `g_input_buf` `LP2` buffer using `LPDT` framing, `boot.mjs` currently caps individual `EffectResp` bodies at `64,000` bytes (`effectLimits = 65536`). Keep REST responses paginated below `64 KB`, or stream larger downloads in chunks via `.httpStream` / `@lakeport/transport`.\n</Callout>\n\nNext, read [Packages, Reservoir, and bundle size](/docs/reservoir-and-bundle-size).\n"
  | .reservoirAndBundleSize => "---\ntitle: \"Reservoir modules and bundle size\"\nsummary: \"Pull Lean packages from Reservoir or local paths, erase proofs to zero bytes, and verify dead-code removal with .ilean reachability.\"\nsection: \"Modules and capabilities\"\norder: 11\nreadingMinutes: 7\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Reservoir modules and bundle size\n\nIn the JavaScript ecosystem, importing one utility from an npm package often drags along transitive polyfills and top-level module side effects that bundlers cannot safely tree-shake.\n\nPure Lean 4 modules have no implicit top-level side effects and expose a complete static declaration call graph in `.ilean` artifacts. You can pull rich packages from [Reservoir](https://reservoir.lean-lang.org) (Lean's package registry) or local workspace paths in `lakefile.toml`, include extensive `*Laws.lean` proof suites, and pay WebAssembly bytes **only** for runtime functions reachable from `App.app`.\n\n```toml title=\"Adding packages in lakefile.toml\"\n[[require]]\nname = \"lakeport-mdx\"\npath = \"packages/lakeport-mdx\"\n```\n\n## Five stages of proof and dead-code stripping\n\nDuring `lakeport build web --release`, unused code and proof machinery drop out across five stages:\n\n1. **`Prop` erasure (`0 B`):** Every value of type `Prop` (such as `MemoCell.coherent`, `SafeSlug`, or equality witnesses) is erased inside the Lean compiler before IR emission. Proof size never increases function code size.\n2. **Leaf law module omission (`[proof-only, 0 B C]`):** Modules like `WorkbenchLaws.lean` and `SiteLaws.lean` are kernel-checked during `lakeport check` and skipped completely when invoking `lean -c` for C codegen.\n3. **`meta` phase separation (`0 B`):** Compile-time macros and syntax elaborators execute while producing `.olean` files and never emit runtime C symbols.\n4. **Per-declaration C sections + LTO (`-ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Oz -flto -Wl,--gc-sections`):** Unreferenced C functions and static tables are garbage-collected by `wasm-ld`.\n5. **Binaryen whole-program pass (`wasm-opt -Oz`):** Compacts WebAssembly locals, folds constants, and inlines small wrappers across the linked module.\n\n## Interactive package & proof-stripping explorer\n\nToggle optional packages and usage profiles below to inspect how proof lines (`0 B` at runtime) and declaration reachability shape the compiled output:\n\n<DeadCodeExplorer />\n\n## Catching dead code early with .ilean reachability\n\nDuring `lakeport check`, Lakeport runs a breadth-first search over `.ilean` reference graphs starting from `App.app` (`proofAnchoredDeclarations`). If you delete a retired `OrderSchema.v1` branch and leave behind helper functions referenced **only** by an orphaned theorem in `*Laws.lean` (and nowhere in your runtime app), `lakeport check` warns you immediately so dead helpers do not linger in your repository.\n\nNext, see [CLI and MCP workflow reference](/docs/cli-and-mcp).\n"
  | .cliAndMcp => "---\ntitle: \"CLI and MCP workflow reference\"\nsummary: \"Command reference for lakeport check, dev, build, doctor, and mcp, plus CI negative checks for exhaustive union handlers.\"\nsection: \"Modules and capabilities\"\norder: 12\nreadingMinutes: 6\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# CLI and MCP workflow reference\n\nEverything in Lakeport runs through one native binary (`lakeport`) without external Webpack, Vite, Babel, or PostCSS configuration files.\n\n## Core CLI commands\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `lakeport init <dir>` | Scaffolds `lakefile.toml`, starter `src/App.lp`, `src/app.css`, and coding-agent instructions. |\n| `lakeport check [dir]` | Verifies global and scoped CSS classes, lowers `.lp` files with 1:1 line maps, checks all `.lean` and `*Laws.lean` theorems (`0 sorryAx`), and runs `.ilean` reachability + zero-`Float` audits. |\n| `lakeport dev [dir]` | Runs a local development server watching `.lp`, `.css`, and `.lean` files with structured reload diagnostics. |\n| `lakeport build web [dir] --release` | Runs `check`, compiles reachable C11 modules to `wasm32` (`-Oz -flto --gc-sections` + `wasm-opt -Oz`), and pre-renders all `ssgRoutes` to `dist/web/`. |\n| `lakeport build <target> [dir]` | Builds experimental native host targets (`linux`, `macos`, `windows`, `ios`, `android`; platform UI gates remain open). |\n| `lakeport doctor` | Verifies `lean`, `lake`, `emcc`, `wasm-opt`, `node`, and optional native SDKs on the host machine. |\n| `lakeport mcp` | Launches the Model Context Protocol stdio server so coding agents can invoke structured compiler checks directly. |\n\n## Structured diagnostics over lakeport mcp\n\nConfiguring Claude Code, Codex, or Cursor to run `lakeport mcp` gives your agent machine-readable JSON diagnostics instead of raw terminal text:\n\n```toml title=\"Registering lakeport mcp\"\n[mcp_servers.lakeport]\ncommand = \"lakeport\"\nargs = [\"mcp\"]\n```\n\nEach diagnostic entry reports:\n\n- Exact source path (`src/Widgets.lp`, `src/app.css`, or `src/OutboxLaws.lean`) mapped back from lowered `.lean` coordinates.\n- 1-indexed line and column numbers.\n- Category code (`css-global`, `css-module`, `tsx-tag`, `tsx-attr`, `lean`, `float-ban`, or `dead-decl`).\n- Unsolved Lean proof goals when a `*Laws.lean` theorem fails after a reducer edit.\n\n## Negative union checks in CI\n\nTo guarantee that nobody introduces a catch-all `_ =>` wildcard arm into a critical schema union, add a small negative test in CI (like `tests/check-negative-v3.py` in `lakeport-www`): copy the workspace to a temp folder, inject a dummy `| v3` constructor into your schema enum, run `lakeport check`, and assert that compilation **fails** naming the missing `v3` case.\n\nNext, dive into **How Lakeport is built** starting with [Architecture overview](/docs/architecture-overview).\n"
  | .architectureOverview => "---\ntitle: \"Architecture overview\"\nsummary: \"How Lakeport's 17 packages divide responsibilities across the CLI compiler, Lean SDKs, frozen 4-node ViewApi kernel, LP2 C seam, and host shells.\"\nsection: \"How Lakeport is built\"\norder: 13\nreadingMinutes: 9\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Architecture overview\n\nIf you open the Lakeport repository (`packages/`), there are `17` packages written in Lean 4, C11, ESM JavaScript (`.mjs`), Java, Swift, and C++ (with zero TypeScript, Rust, or Kotlin). This chapter gives contributors a clear mental map of how those packages fit together, what principles govern the design, and why the core kernel boundary looks the way it does.\n\n## Four-tier system diagram\n\n<ArchLayersDiagram />\n\n## The 17 packages at a glance\n\n| Layer | Packages in `packages/` | Role in the system |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **1. Compiler & CLI (`1`)** | `lakeport-cli` | Native Lean 4 CLI (`~21.5k` LOC Lean + `1.7k` LOC `cli_ffi.c` + `5.8k` LOC `web-runtime/*.mjs`): `.lp` JSX lowerer, CSS parser/scoper, `.lpcss` emitter, wave compiler, `wasm32` C rewriter, SSG runner, and `lakeport mcp`. |\n| **2. Verified Kernel (`5`)** | `lakeport-core`, `lakeport-signals`, `lakeport-codec`, `lakeport-session`, `lakeport-web` | Minimal 4-constructor `ViewApi`, `Epoch`/`Ticket` admission (`34` theorems), fuel-bounded `Term` signals (`82+` theorems), `LPDT` v1 binary data codec, `Supervisor` session model, and `StyledRoot` + `lp2_shim.c` (`643` LOC C11). |\n| **3. Auto-Imported SDKs (`5`)** | `lakeport-style`, `lakeport-router`, `lakeport-effects`, `lakeport-json`, `lakeport-xml` | Prepended automatically to app builds by `Toolchain.lean`: `StyledView` (`15` HTML tags), `MemoCell`, `LPST` `O(N log N)` style diff, `LawfulUrlCodec`, `18` `EffectReq` commands, `Outbox`, and pure-Lean JSON/XML codecs. |\n| **4. Capability Plugins (`3`)** | `lakeport-plugin-transport`, `lakeport-plugin-media-device`, `lakeport-plugin-on-device-ai` | Optional typed `pluginInvoke` / `pluginSubscribe` bridges for SSE/multipart streaming, camera/BLE/location/reachability, and local model inference. |\n| **5. Native Host Shells (`3`)** | `lakeport-desktop`, `lakeport-android`, `lakeport-ios` | Imperative host renderers: Linux GTK4/PangoCairo (`15.9k` LOC C11), macOS AppKit Swift, Win32 Direct2D C++, Android JNI + Java `.lpcss`, and iOS UIKit Swift. |\n\n## Core design principles\n\nFour engineering rules explain most of what you see across `packages/`:\n\n1. **Pure deterministic core, imperative edges:** Application state, routing, caching (`MemoCell`), and styling trees execute as pure functions inside Lean with `Float` banned in runtime app modules so `wasm32` and 64-bit native runs stay bit-identical. All I/O (`fetch`, DOM mutation, timers, storage) lives outside Lean in the host shell.\n2. **Zero-cost compile-time proofs:** Verification lives in `Prop` fields and leaf `*Laws.lean` modules (`[proof-only, 0 B C]`). Proofs gate compilation and erase before C11 emission.\n3. **Narrow binary C ABI (`LP2` v4) instead of per-widget FFI:** Rather than binding hundreds of DOM or GTK functions via Lean `@[extern]` calls, Lean and the host communicate strictly by copying compact binary frames (`LPRQ`, `Reply2` + `LPST`, `LPSN`) across three static byte buffers in `lp2_shim.c`.\n4. **Line-preserving source lowering over macros:** `.lp` TSX files lower 1:1 per line into `.lean` files before Lean starts, keeping compiler errors fast and avoiding complex Lean macro tracebacks for frontend authors.\n\n## Why ViewApi and LP2 froze early (and what that cost)\n\nEarly in Lakeport's development, `lakeport-core` froze `ViewApi` at **four constructors** (`.text`, `.button`, `.input`, `.group`) and froze the `643`-line `lp2_shim.c` C bridge so kernel proofs (`lakeport-core`, `lakeport-session`, `lakeport-web`) would have a rock-solid target while higher-level features grew above them.\n\nUnderstanding both sides of that decision makes the rest of the codebase click immediately:\n\n- **What freezing `ViewApi` bought:** Reconciliation, `Epoch`/`Ticket` command admission, pure capacity rollback (`RejectX.capacity`), and `LPST` style trailer equivalence (`diffFastWithIx_eq_diff`) are proved end to end with `0 sorryAx` and a tiny C trusted computing base.\n- **What freezing `ViewApi` cost:** Every richer UI concept added later (`<img>`, `<svg>`, `<textarea>`, disabled inputs, CSS classes, `--*` variables, `<RouterShell>` URLs, FLIP animations, and drag-and-drop) had to be **tunneled through `NodeStyle` and `(String × String)` `data-lp-*` attributes** inside the `LPST` binary trailer, and `<button>` remained the sole carrier of `onClick`.\n\nNext, read [Compiler pipeline and LP2 runtime](/docs/compiler-and-runtime) to trace a `.lp` file all the way to WebAssembly and DOM commits.\n"
  | .compilerAndRuntime => "---\ntitle: \"Compiler pipeline and LP2 runtime\"\nsummary: \"Step-by-step walkthrough of Build.lean, LpTsx lowering, 32-bit Wasm C rewrites, LP2 dual-buffer dispatch, LPST diffing, SSG, and HMR.\"\nsection: \"How Lakeport is built\"\norder: 14\nreadingMinutes: 10\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Compiler pipeline and LP2 runtime\n\nWhen you run `lakeport check` or `lakeport build web --release`, orchestration starts in `packages/lakeport-cli/Lakeport/Cli/Build.lean`. This chapter walks through each compiler stage and the runtime `LP2` v4 frame loop so you can debug or extend any stage with confidence.\n\n## Seven-stage build pipeline\n\n<BuildPipelineDiagram />\n\n### Clever trick 1: 1:1 line-preserving .lp lowering (LpTsx.lean)\n\nIf lowering `<div class=\"card hero\">` allocated a fresh `List ClassName` on every call to `view`, rendering would churn heap memory. Yet if `LpTsx.lean` inserted top-level `private def _lp_ns_0 : NodeStyle := ...` lines at the top of the generated `.lean` file, every Lean type error below it would report shifted line numbers!\n\n`LpTsx.lean` (`2,146` lines) solves both problems at once: it hoists all static `_lp_ns_k : NodeStyle` definitions **onto the single final `import` line** (separated by semicolons), preserving exact 1:1 line numbers between `Foo.lp` and `Foo.lean` while keeping static styles zero-allocation per frame.\n\n### Clever trick 2: 32-bit wasm32 C literal rewrites and lean_string_data\n\nLean 4 emits C code assuming 64-bit `size_t` in a few subtle places, while browser WebAssembly (`wasm32-unknown-emscripten`) uses 32-bit pointers and 32-bit boxed `lean_box` scalars (`31` value bits). Lakeport applies two targeted fixes before invoking `emcc`:\n\n1. **`WasmNatLit.lean` (`[2^31, 2^63)` literals):** On 64-bit hosts, Lean compiles `Nat` literals in `[2^31, 2^63)` as `lean_box(NULL)`, which truncates silently on 32-bit `wasm32`! `WasmNatLit.lean` rewrites those C tokens into `lean_cstr_to_nat(\"N\")`, then runs an **exact textual inverse pass** to verify byte-for-byte that nothing outside those literals was altered.\n2. **`-Wl,--wrap=lean_string_data` (`cli_ffi.c` / Web linker):** In Lean 4.35's runtime headers on 32-bit targets, `lean_alloc_sarray` leaves `m_size == 0` when allocating `\"\"` (`lean_mk_string_unchecked(\"\", 0, 0)`), which shares bit representation with `lean_box_uint32(0)` and causes `lean_string_cstr(\"\")` to read garbage memory. Wrapping `lean_string_data` intercepts that exact 32-bit edge case cleanly.\n\n## Runtime LP2 v4 dispatch and two-phase command release\n\nAt runtime, the compiled Lean module links against `packages/lakeport-web/host/lp2_shim.c` (`643` LOC C11, `18` exported symbols) and talks to `packages/lakeport-cli/web-runtime/lp2-abi.mjs` + `boot.mjs`:\n\n<Lp2AbiDiagram />\n\n### Capacity-safe rollback and HEAPU8 growth safety\n\n- **Pure rollback on buffer overflow:** `lp2_shim.c` holds static buffers (`64 KiB` `g_input_buf`, `1 MiB` `g_pub_buf`, `1 MiB` `g_held_buf`). `StyledRoot.step` evaluates your reducer and view under `wideCaps` (`2 GiB - 1`) to measure exact serialized byte lengths; if the frame exceeds the caller's `(publicCap, heldCap)`, Lean **discards the new state purely** and returns `RejectX.capacity(needPub, needHeld)` so `boot.mjs` enlarges its cap budget and retries cleanly.\n- **Wasm memory growth safety:** Because `-sALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH=1` detaches the underlying `ArrayBuffer` whenever Wasm grows linear memory during `lp2_dispatch`, `lp2-abi.mjs` re-reads `module.HEAPU8` *after* `lp2_dispatch` returns and verifies `lp2_output_status(token)` before and after copying output slices.\n- **Proved `O(N log N)` style trailer diff (`LpstWire.lean`):** Instead of resending unchanged CSS class lists and `data-*` attributes on every click, Lean sorts node style entries and emits only changed `NodeId` records (`diffFastWithIx`), proved bit-identical to the reference quadratic diff (`diffFastWithIx_eq_diff`).\n- **Two-phase command `ack`:** User events do not fire `fetch` or `localStorage` inside `lp2_dispatch`. Commands stage inside `g_held_buf` (`LPSN`) while the session enters `awaitAck`. Only after the frame installs does `#runPlumbing()` send `ack(gen, commit)`, releasing the `EffectReq` batch to the host.\n\n## How dev-server reload works today\n\nIn `lakeport dev`, Lean state lives inside compiled Wasm linear memory with opaque Lean closures and constructors. Rather than serializing arbitrary Lean heap graphs across schema changes, `boot.mjs` currently records an **inbound action log** (`press`, `edit`, `navigate`) and replays that log into the newly compiled `.wasm` binary on hot reload (falling back to fresh `initial` state if an action tag changed).\n\nNext, read [Code quality and contributor guide](/docs/contributor-guide) for an unfiltered assessment of what is strong in the codebase today and where tech debt needs cleanup.\n"
  | .contributorGuide => "---\ntitle: \"Code quality and contributor guide\"\nsummary: \"A candid engineering review of Lakeport's codebase: what is solid, where the tech debt and landmines live, and where to contribute.\"\nsection: \"How Lakeport is built\"\norder: 15\nreadingMinutes: 10\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Code quality and contributor guide\n\nWhen software is built fast with heavy coding-agent assistance, it is easy to end up with a system that passes smoke tests while hiding tangled internals. If you are considering contributing to Lakeport (or evaluating how trustworthy its implementation really is), you deserve a candid engineering answer to a blunt question: **is the code actually good, or is it just working?**\n\nThe honest verdict: **the verified Lean kernel, codecs, SDK proofs, and Wasm C compiler passes are solid, well-tested engineering; the surrounding CLI build plumbing, `data-lp-*` view tunneling, and cross-platform host shells carry real prototype scaffolding and duplication that contributors should clean up.**\n\n## What is strong today (keep and build on)\n\n1. **Real `0 sorryAx` theorems on production code (`lakeport-core`, `lakeport-style`, `lakeport-effects`, `lakeport-router`, `lakeport-json`):** The proofs across the kernel and SDKs are not toy examples bolted on the side. `LpstWire.diffFastWithIx_eq_diff` proves the production `O(N log N)` binary style diff equals its specification; `WirePack` proves bijective escaping; `MemoCell` proves zero-stale-cache coherence; `LakeportJson` proves lossless decimal and round-trip laws without a single `Float`.\n2. **Defensive `wasm32` C compilation (`WasmNatLit.lean` & `lean_string_data`):** Diagnosing Lean 4.35's 64-bit `lean_box` truncation and 32-bit `m_size == 0` empty-string bug, and verifying `WasmNatLit` with an exact textual inverse pass on every C file, prevented subtle memory corruption bugs in WebAssembly.\n3. **1:1 line-preserving `.lp` lowering (`LpTsx.lean`):** Hoisting static `_lp_ns_k : NodeStyle` constants onto the final `import` line gives zero-allocation static styles while keeping compiler line numbers aligned with `.lp` source files.\n4. **Closed-schema native supply-chain checks (`NativeInputs.lean`):** Third-party native dependencies require explicit provenance, license allowlists, and SHA-256 verification before packaging.\n\n## Where the rough edges and tech debt live\n\nContributors jumping into `packages/` should watch out for six concrete rough spots:\n\n### 1. The frozen ViewApi pinch point (<button>, gestures, and data-lp-* strings)\n\nBecause `ViewApi` froze early at `.text`, `.button (label : String)`, `.input`, and `.group`, `LpTsx.lean` (`lines 765, 1065`) rejects `onClick` on non-`<button>` elements and forbids element children inside `<button>`. That forces every clickable card or icon button to use `.hit-overlay` CSS hacks, and leaves **zero touch swipe, pan/pinch, scroll, or focus/blur events** reaching Lean.\n\nFreezing `ViewApi` also forced `<svg>`, `<img>`, `<native-view>`, `<input disabled>`, `<RouterShell url>`, and FLIP animations to tunnel through untyped `(String × String)` `dataAttrs` in `NodeStyle`. As a result:\n\n- `dom-renderer.mjs` runs `root.querySelectorAll('[data-flip-id]')` and `getBoundingClientRect()` twice on **every** committed frame even when an app uses zero FLIP animations.\n- `boot.mjs` linearly scans the entire `StyleTable` on every commit just to locate `data-lp-url`.\n\n### 2. Four-way host engine duplication (~30k+ LOC)\n\nBecause the `LP2` boundary sits below layout and style resolution, `Wire2` decoding, `LPST` style patching, `.lpcss` evaluation, and Flex/Grid layout were reimplemented separately in Web JS (`5.8k` LOC), Linux C11 (`15.9k` LOC in `lakeport-desktop/linux/`), Android Java, Apple Swift, and Windows C++. Subtle behavioral drift already exists between them (for example, Web sets `data-drag-over=\"true\"` while Linux GTK sets `data-drop-over=\"true\"`). Moving layout or shared wire/style state machines into C11 or Lean would cut tens of thousands of duplicated lines.\n\n### 3. Single-machine paths and doc SHA-256 pins in Toolchain.lean\n\n`packages/lakeport-cli/Lakeport/Cli/Toolchain.lean` currently hardcodes workstation paths (`/home/ubuntu/.elan/...`, `/home/ubuntu/emsdk/...`), internal SSH build hosts (`macos-vm`, `windows-dev`), and SHA-256 hashes of internal markdown specification files (`128..132` and `140-python-retirement-manifest.json`). Editing one character in those markdown docs breaks `lakeport doctor` until the hash literal in `Toolchain.lean` is updated, and external contributors cloning onto macOS or another Linux username hit path assumptions immediately.\n\n### 4. Raw C constructor slot mutations and singleton lp2_shim.c state\n\n- `ApkPackager.lean` and `DesktopPackager.lean` emit C wrappers that mutate `StyledApp` fields by raw constructor slot index (`lean_ctor_set(..., 13, ...)` and `14`), which will silently corrupt memory if `StyledApp`'s field order ever changes.\n- `lp2_shim.c` uses process-global static arrays (`g_input_buf`, `g_pub_buf`, `g_held_buf`, and a 2-slot `g_abandoned` array), limiting native hosts to one Lakeport instance per OS process.\n- Long-session Wasm heap retention gate `S3b-heap-live-160-equals-40` remains open in `docs/implementation/`.\n\n### 5. Silent 64 KB effect truncation and URL query stripping\n\n`boot.mjs` silently slices outbound/inbound `EffectResp` bodies to `64,000` bytes (`sliceUtf8(body, 64000)`) instead of surfacing a typed `.payloadTooLarge` error or auto-chunking across frames, and `LakeportRouter.Url.parseUrl` discards `?query` and `#hash` strings.\n\n### 6. Fixture fallbacks in native packagers\n\nWhen GTK4 headers or prebuilt iOS `.a` archives are missing on a build host, parts of the Linux and iOS packagers can fall back to compiling stub fixture binaries rather than failing loudly unless strict gate flags are passed.\n\n## High-impact starter projects for contributors\n\n| Area | Key files | Concrete improvement |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Portable CLI discovery** | `packages/lakeport-cli/Lakeport/Cli/Toolchain.lean` | Replace `/home/ubuntu/...` and SSH host constants with `PATH` / `ELAN_HOME` / `EMSDK` env lookup and remove doc SHA-256 pins from `doctor`. |\n| **Pressable container & gesture design** | `packages/lakeport-core/`, `LpTsx.lean`, `dom-renderer.mjs` | Add a fifth `Pressable` / interactive container constructor (or structured gesture channel) to `ViewApi` and update kernel proofs so clickable cards and swipe gestures do not need `.hit-overlay`. |\n| **O(1) frame fast paths** | `packages/lakeport-cli/web-runtime/dom-renderer.mjs`, `boot.mjs` | Track `[data-flip-id]` and `lp-url` nodes incrementally when `LPST` diffs arrive instead of scanning the full DOM / `StyleTable` every commit. |\n| **Structured query/hash routing** | `packages/lakeport-router/src/LakeportRouter/Url.lean` | Extend `UrlCodec` and `LawfulUrlCodec` to preserve typed `?query` parameters and `#hash` anchors (enabling deep-linked heading scroll in docs). |\n| **Explicit >64 KB HTTP responses** | `packages/lakeport-effects/`, `web-runtime/boot.mjs` | Replace silent `64,000`-byte slicing with multi-frame chunking or an explicit `EffectError.responseTooLarge` constructor. |\n\nEvery contribution that touches `lakeport-core`, `lakeport-style`, `lakeport-router`, `lakeport-effects`, or `lakeport-json` is checked by `lakeport check` (`0 sorryAx`), so you can refactor boldly knowing the Lean kernel will catch broken invariants immediately.\n"

